They’re related, but not the same.

Romanticizing means transforming something into a more beautiful, meaningful, dramatic, or desirable story than the full reality warrants. It tends to edit out costs and contradictions. You romanticize war, poverty, heartbreak, madness, the tortured artist, the outlaw, the past.

Fetishizing means narrowing attention onto some trait, object, identity, experience, or symbol until it becomes disproportionately charged with desire, fascination, status, or meaning. The thing can stop being encountered as a whole and instead becomes a token for what you want it to represent.

So:

romanticization = narrative idealization

fetishization = selective fixation + symbolic/desire amplification

And they can stack:

real thing → select attractive features → suppress inconvenient features → amplify symbolic meaning → desire the constructed version

That gives you romantic fetishization: you’re no longer attracted primarily to the actual thing; you’re attracted to the story and symbolic payload you’ve attached to it.

There’s an interesting mirror failure here too. Romanticizing tends to make the whole prettier by omission; fetishizing tends to make a part larger than the whole. Both distort scale, just in opposite directions.